On Tue, Jun 24, 2014 at 3:15 PM, Alex Burka <durka42@gmail.com> wrote:

>  Hmm, okay. So is it that {ibliardo} and {bolrbliardo} are pronounced with
> a syllabic L, like {.ibl,iardo}/{bol,rbl,iardo}, as opposed to {bli,iardo}
> with more of a BL cluster?
>

Ths canonical syllabifications would be ".i,bl,iar,do",  "bo,lr,bl,iar.do"
and "bli.iar,do". Consonantal syllables are always exactly two consonants
long, with the second consonant being one of l/m/n/r, so there are only 64
possible consonantal syllables. A brivla cannot start with a consonantal
syllable.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

